From ec510d05732a88f2aee3364a1cbc7d932e0a3e97 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: albanread <albanread@googlemail.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 19:41:37 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] libgame: BGamePane, a window whose pixels are palette indices
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The world buffer lives in the display's surface pool, so the bytes a
program writes are the bytes the host's GPU reads: nothing between them
is copied, converted or composited by app_server. Index 0 is
transparent, 1 to 15 come from the row's own sixteen colours and 16 to
255 from a global palette.

It is a BDirectWindow, for the half of BDirectWindow that is worth
having. The pointer it offers is useless — a direct client can write the
front buffer but has no way to present it — while the clip list and
screen geometry it delivers on every move, resize and hide are exactly
what a host-side compositor needs. DirectConnected forwards them and
ignores the pixels.

Drawing is here, on the CPU, because a byte per pixel in write-back
memory is faster to bang on than to ask anything else about; sprites are
the host's, because scaling and rotation are not.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
